{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "t-sql-correctness",
  "domain": "tsql-correctness-and-patterns",
  "value": ["corrects-a-wrong-assumption"],
  "correction": "Asked for SQL against Azure SQL Database, an agent writes statements that succeed and return the wrong answer. Comparing a nullable column with = or <> or NOT IN is three-valued logic, not two: WHERE status = @p finds nothing when @p is NULL, WHERE status <> 'shipped' silently drops every row whose status is NULL, and NOT IN over a subquery holding one NULL returns the empty set rather than a set short by one. Aggregates skip nulls, so AVG divides by the non-null count and COUNT(v) disagrees with COUNT(*), and measured on 2026-09-03 against EngineEdition 5 through sqlcmd -I -m-1 the engine says nothing at all about it: message 8153 is defined in sys.messages at severity 10 and was never raised, while Microsoft Learn's SET ANSI_WARNINGS page states a warning is generated. Integer division truncates because / returns the higher-precedence operand's type. ISNULL returns the type of its FIRST argument and converts the second into it, so ISNULL over an NVARCHAR(3) silently returns 'abc' where COALESCE returns 'abcdef'. A string declared with no length is one character, and thirty in a cast. And QUOTED_IDENTIFIER is decided at parse time, so the same double-quoted text is a string literal in one session and an identifier in another: the ODBC and OLE DB drivers set it ON at connect, the container's own sqlcmd leaves it OFF. The second failure is the loud one, PostgreSQL and MySQL syntax under a T-SQL name, and the third is overcorrecting into syntax a decade old when the engine has shipped the ANSI double pipe operator, IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, GREATEST and STRING_AGG.",
    "value_probe": {
      "gate": "The answer explains all three wrong results as consequences of how the engine evaluates comparisons and aggregates when a column holds no value, and returns rewritten queries that produce the intended numbers. An answer that blames the data or the application, or that corrects only one of the three, has not reached it.",
      "task": "Our order dashboard on Azure SQL Database disagrees with itself and nobody is getting an error message. dbo.orders has id, customer_id, status and discount_pct, and dbo.cancelled_orders has order_id. Three things are wrong.\n\nThe \"not shipped\" tile runs:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.orders WHERE status <> 'shipped';\n```\n\nIt returns 4180. The table has 9000 rows and the shipped tile returns 3100, so 1720 orders are in neither tile.\n\nThe active order list runs:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM dbo.orders WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT order_id FROM dbo.cancelled_orders);\n```\n\nIt returns zero rows, and only about forty orders have ever been cancelled.\n\nAnd `SELECT AVG(discount_pct) FROM dbo.orders` does not agree with `SELECT SUM(discount_pct) / COUNT(*) FROM dbo.orders`.\n\nExplain what is happening in each case and give me the corrected queries.",
      "states": [
        "A comparison against a column with no value evaluates to unknown rather than true, so the filter excludes the row instead of keeping it.",
        "The second query returns nothing because the subquery contains at least one row with no value, which makes the predicate unknown for every candidate row.",
        "The average and the manual sum over row count differ because the aggregate skips rows with no value while the row count does not.",
        "The engine accepts all three statements and returns a wrong answer rather than reporting anything to the client."
      ],
      "refutes": [
        "Azure SQL Database raises a warning to the client when an aggregate eliminates rows that hold no value, so this discrepancy would already have been visible in the query output.",
        "NOT IN and NOT EXISTS return the same rows over a subquery whose column can hold no value, so that rewrite is a matter of style rather than correctness.",
        "An inequality comparison already includes rows whose column holds no value, so the first tile is counting correctly and the missing 1720 rows must have been deleted or filtered somewhere else.",
        "The rows are missing because of a collation difference, a data type mismatch or an implicit conversion in the WHERE clause."
      ]
    },
  "posture": ["read", "write"],
  // Held at draft below a preview ceiling, on purpose. The value measurement of
  // 2026-09-04 returned NEITHER: the model already answers this skill's own task
  // correctly without it, so the skill changes nothing about the answer. Its
  // engine probes still pass. Nothing here is broken and nothing here is
  // unverified. This is an author declaring below the ceiling, which the ladder
  // permits, and it is not the skill failing. Do not raise this field back to
  // clear the promotion backlog. It moves when the skill is rewritten to say
  // something the model does not already know and a fresh measurement shows
  // lift, and not before.
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "design-azure-sql-schema",
    "t-sql-upserts-merge",
    "t-sql-json-and-openjson",
    "t-sql-regex-and-new-functions",
    "t-sql-error-handling",
    "t-sql-programmability-objects",
    "post-migration-compatibility-level"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a database with one table carrying a nullable column and one carrying an identity column",
      "a copy of the identity table with an enabled AFTER INSERT trigger"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "no predicate compares a nullable column or parameter with = or <> against a value that can be NULL, without IS NULL, IS NOT DISTINCT FROM or an explicit OR col IS NULL",
      "no NOT IN names a subquery over a nullable column; NOT EXISTS is used instead",
      "every average, ratio or percentage over a nullable column states whether NULL means zero or means excluded",
      "no division intended to produce a fraction has integer operands on both sides",
      "ISNULL is not substituted for COALESCE, or the reverse, without stating the intended return type",
      "every character variable, parameter and cast carries an explicit length",
      "the generated pagination uses ORDER BY with a unique tiebreaker plus OFFSET and FETCH, and does not combine TOP with OFFSET",
      "every insert that needs its new key uses OUTPUT, with INTO when the target has an enabled trigger",
      "no generated statement contains LIMIT, RETURNING, SERIAL, ILIKE, NOW(), true, false, ON CONFLICT or USE",
      "string literals use single quotes and double quotes appear only around identifiers"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_null_cmp (id INT, status NVARCHAR(20) NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_null_cmp VALUES (1, N'shipped'), (2, N'held'), (3, NULL);\nDECLARE @p NVARCHAR(20) = NULL;\nSELECT SUM(CASE WHEN status = @p THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS matched_by_equals,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN status <> N'shipped' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS not_shipped,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN status IS NOT DISTINCT FROM @p THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS matched_null_safe\nFROM dbo.probe_null_cmp;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [[0, 1, 1]]
        },
        "why": "The claim the whole skill turns on, made executable in one row. Three rows exist, one of them NULL. Equality against a NULL parameter matches nothing, which is the documented ANSI_NULLS ON behaviour where every comparison against NULL is UNKNOWN and WHERE keeps only TRUE. The middle column is the one that reaches production: 'everything not shipped' is 1, not 2, because the NULL row is neither equal nor unequal. The third column is the fix, and IS NOT DISTINCT FROM is documented to return true or false and never unknown. Microsoft Learn: SET ANSI_NULLS truth table, and IS [NOT] DISTINCT FROM."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_assigned (customer_id INT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_assigned VALUES (1), (NULL);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_customers (id INT NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_customers VALUES (1), (2), (3);\nSELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_customers c\n        WHERE c.id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM dbo.probe_assigned)) AS by_not_in,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_customers c\n        WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dbo.probe_assigned a WHERE a.customer_id = c.id)) AS by_not_exists;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [[0, 2]]
        },
        "why": "A single NULL in the subquery makes the NOT IN predicate UNKNOWN for every candidate row, so the answer is the empty set rather than a set short by one. Microsoft Learn's IN page carries this as a Caution: 'Any null values returned by subquery or expression that are compared to test_expression using IN or NOT IN return UNKNOWN'. The second column is the remedy the skill prescribes, proved to return the two customers a reader expects. Asserting both in one row is the point: the contrast is the correction, and a probe that measured only NOT IN would leave the fix unmeasured."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_readings (v INT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_readings VALUES (10), (NULL), (20);\nSELECT SUM(v) AS total, COUNT(*) AS all_rows, COUNT(v) AS non_null_rows, AVG(v) AS mean\nFROM dbo.probe_readings;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [[30, 3, 2, 15]]
        },
        "why": "The mean is not the total over the row count. AVG divides 30 by the two non-null rows and returns 15, where a reader reading COUNT(*) expects 10. Measured 2026-09-03 on EngineEdition 5, where every one of these four values came back with no message of any kind. COUNT(v) beside COUNT(*) in the same row is what makes the divisor visible. Sourcing note: the first draft of this cited a NULL semantics page that turned out to be Azure Databricks rather than this engine, so the claim now rests on the measurement and on the aggregate list in Microsoft Learn's SET ANSI_WARNINGS page."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SET ANSI_WARNINGS ON;\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_agg_silent (v INT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_agg_silent VALUES (10), (NULL), (20);\nSELECT (SELECT SUM(v) FROM dbo.probe_agg_silent) AS total,\n       (SELECT COUNT(v) FROM dbo.probe_agg_silent) AS non_null_rows,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dbo.probe_agg_silent) AS all_rows,\n       @@OPTIONS & 8 AS ansi_warnings_bit,\n       (SELECT MAX(severity) FROM sys.messages WHERE message_id = 8153) AS msg_8153_severity;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [[30, 2, 3, 8, 10]]
        },
        "why": "This probe previously asserted warning 8153 and it FAILED against a real engine on 2026-09-03, EngineEdition 5, ProductVersion 12.0.2000.8, through sqlcmd -I -m-1, the same invocation that surfaces Msg 1945 for design-azure-sql-schema. The sum returned 30 and no message at all. So the probe now asserts what the engine does. The ansi_warnings_bit column is why the result can be trusted rather than dismissed as a session that had warnings off: @@OPTIONS & 8 is 8, so ANSI_WARNINGS genuinely is ON. The msg_8153_severity column is the sharpest part: message 8153 is defined in sys.messages at severity 10 and is simply never raised, which is the skill's own thesis about statements that succeed and return a wrong answer, stated by the engine's own catalog. Microsoft Learn disagrees, and the disagreement is the finding rather than something to smooth over: SET ANSI_WARNINGS says 'When set to ON, if null values appear in aggregate functions, such as SUM, AVG, MAX, MIN, STDEV, STDEVP, VAR, VARP, or COUNT, a warning message is generated', and ALTER DATABASE SET options repeats it. On this engine no warning is generated. COUNT(v) at 2 against COUNT(*) at 3 carries the divergence in the same row, so this probe stands alone if the one above it is read without it."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT 7 / 2 AS integer_division, CAST(7.0 / 2 AS DECIMAL(4,1)) AS decimal_division;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [[3, "3.5"]]
        },
        "why": "Microsoft Learn's / (Division) page: the operator returns the data type of the argument with the higher precedence, and 'if an integer dividend is divided by an integer divisor, the result is an integer that has any fractional part of the result truncated'. No warning is raised, so a percentage or a rate built from two integer columns is simply wrong. The second column is cast to DECIMAL(4,1) rather than left as the raw literal type so the printed text is stable across builds rather than a probe about decimal scale."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DECLARE @short NVARCHAR(3) = NULL;\nSELECT ISNULL(@short, N'abcdef') AS isnull_result, COALESCE(@short, N'abcdef') AS coalesce_result;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [["abc", "abcdef"]]
        },
        "why": "ISNULL and COALESCE are treated as interchangeable and are not. Microsoft Learn's ISNULL page: the function returns the same type as check_expression, and 'replacement_value can be truncated if replacement_value is longer than check_expression'. COALESCE follows the CASE rules and returns the type with the highest precedence, so it returns all six characters. Swapping one for the other as a style edit changes the value returned, silently."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DECLARE @v NVARCHAR = N'abcdef';\nSELECT @v AS declared_without_length,\n       LEN(CAST(REPLICATE(CAST(N'a' AS NVARCHAR(MAX)), 40) AS NVARCHAR)) AS cast_without_length;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [["a", 30]]
        },
        "why": "Microsoft Learn's nchar and nvarchar page: 'When n isn't specified in a data definition or variable declaration statement, the default length is 1. When n isn't specified with the CAST function, the default length is 30.' Both halves are asserted in one row because the surprise is that the same omitted length means two different things. The REPLICATE input is cast to NVARCHAR(MAX) first, without which REPLICATE returns nvarchar(4000) and a longer example would measure that limit instead of this one."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER OFF;\nSELECT \"not a column\" AS literal_or_identifier;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [["not a column"]]
        },
        "why": "With QUOTED_IDENTIFIER OFF a double-quoted string is a literal, which Microsoft Learn's SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER page states and demonstrates in its own example B. This is the half a reader meets on the container, whose own sqlcmd leaves the setting OFF while the ODBC and OLE DB drivers set it ON at connect. The pair of probes together is the claim: the same text, two different statements, decided by how the connection was made rather than by anything in the file."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON;\nSELECT \"not a column\" AS literal_or_identifier;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 207
        },
        "why": "The other half. With the setting ON, which Learn calls the default and which every shipping driver sets at connect, the same double-quoted text is read as an object name and the statement fails. Microsoft Learn's 0 to 999 error table gives 207 at severity 16 as 'Invalid column name'. QUOTED_IDENTIFIER is stated in the batch because Learn documents that it takes effect at parse time and that an occurrence inside a top-level ad hoc batch changes parsing from that point on."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_out (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, total DECIMAL(9,2));\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_out_log (id INT NULL);\nEXEC('CREATE TRIGGER dbo.probe_out_trg ON dbo.probe_out AFTER INSERT AS INSERT INTO dbo.probe_out_log (id) SELECT id FROM inserted;');\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_out (total) OUTPUT INSERTED.id VALUES (19.99);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 334
        },
        "why": "The one restriction that separates OUTPUT from PostgreSQL's RETURNING, made executable. Microsoft Learn's OUTPUT clause page: if OUTPUT is specified without INTO, the target of the DML operation cannot have an enabled trigger for that action, and the 0 to 999 error table gives 334 at severity 15 as 'The target table of the DML statement cannot have any enabled triggers if the statement contains an OUTPUT clause without INTO clause'. CREATE TRIGGER must be first in its batch, so it is issued through EXEC rather than by relying on the runner honouring a GO."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_out2 (id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, total DECIMAL(9,2));\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_out2_log (id INT NULL);\nEXEC('CREATE TRIGGER dbo.probe_out2_trg ON dbo.probe_out2 AFTER INSERT AS INSERT INTO dbo.probe_out2_log (id) SELECT id FROM inserted;');\nDECLARE @new TABLE (id INT);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_out2 (total) OUTPUT INSERTED.id INTO @new VALUES (19.99);\nSELECT id FROM @new;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [[1]]
        },
        "why": "The remedy for the probe above, run end to end against the same fixture: the identical insert into the identical triggered table succeeds once OUTPUT captures into a table variable, and the new key is read back. Asserting the key rather than plain success proves the table variable was actually populated and not merely accepted by the parser."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT ISNULL(N'a' || CAST(NULL AS NVARCHAR(10)), N'(null)') AS pipes_result,\n       CONCAT(N'a', CAST(NULL AS NVARCHAR(10))) AS concat_result;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [["(null)", "a"]]
        },
        "why": "Two claims in one statement. That the ANSI double pipe operator exists here at all is the overcorrection half of this skill, and a failure on this probe means the engine predates its July 2025 general availability rather than that the claim is wrong. That it disagrees with CONCAT about NULL is the silent half: || propagates the null while CONCAT treats a null argument as an empty string, so the same name assembled two ways gives two answers."
      }
    ,
          {
            "sql": "SELECT \"not a column\" AS x;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 207
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A double quoted string is an identifier under QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON, which is the setting every driver sets, so the literal a developer typed is read as a column name. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c_trg (id int, v int);\nGO\nCREATE TRIGGER probe_c_trg_ins ON dbo.probe_c_trg AFTER INSERT AS SELECT 1;\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_c_trg (id, v) OUTPUT inserted.id VALUES (1, 1);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 334
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A bare OUTPUT is refused once the target carries an enabled trigger, so a pattern that worked in development breaks the day an audit trigger is added. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c_p (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c_c (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, pid int NOT NULL REFERENCES dbo.probe_c_p(id));\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_c_p (id) OUTPUT inserted.id INTO dbo.probe_c_c (id) VALUES (1);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 332
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. OUTPUT INTO refuses a target on either side of a foreign key, one of the three restrictions that make OUTPUT INTO narrower than it looks. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "SELECT NO_SUCH_FUNCTION_HERE(1) AS x;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 195
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. One of the three numbers a missing function can produce. The skill tells a reader to check the compatibility level before believing the function does not exist, and this is what an unrecognised name looks like. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "SELECT * FROM dbo.probe_c_no_such_object;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 208
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The second of the three, and the one OPENJSON produces below compatibility level 130, which is why the skill says read the level rather than the message. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c_json (id int, j json);\nGO\nSELECT id FROM dbo.probe_c_json WHERE j = N'{}';",
            "expect": {
              "error": 402
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A json column compared with = is a type incompatibility, not a NULL problem, so it is a different failure from the ones this skill's first half is about. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          }
    ,
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c_out_p (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c_out_c (id int NOT NULL, CONSTRAINT ck_probe_c CHECK (id > 0));\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_c_out_p (id) OUTPUT inserted.id INTO dbo.probe_c_out_c (id) VALUES (1);",
            "expect": {
              "error": 333
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The second OUTPUT INTO restriction: the target can carry no enabled CHECK constraint. Together with the foreign key case this is why an audit table written by OUTPUT INTO has to be barer than a normal table. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_c_nulls (v int NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_c_nulls VALUES (10), (NULL), (20);\nGO\nSET ANSI_WARNINGS ON;\nSELECT SUM(v) AS s, COUNT(*) AS c_all, COUNT(v) AS c_val, AVG(v) AS a FROM dbo.probe_c_nulls;",
            "expect": {
              "rows": [
                [
                  30,
                  3,
                  2,
                  15
                ]
              ]
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The aggregate claim, made executable: SUM skips the NULL and returns 30, COUNT(*) counts three rows while COUNT(v) counts two, and AVG divides by two rather than three. What this probe CANNOT assert is the other half of the claim, that message 8153 is never raised: the judge treats a severity 10 message as reportable and not as a failure, so a run where the engine started warning would still pass. That half stays a reading and not a check, and saying so is better than a probe that looks like it covers it. It goes red if any of the four values changes."
          }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the t-sql-correctness skill before writing this query"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "why does this WHERE clause skip rows that have a NULL in that column",
      "why did my NOT IN query return no rows at all",
      "why is this average different from the sum divided by the row count",
      "why does this division return 0 instead of a fraction",
      "write me a paginated query for Azure SQL Database",
      "how do I get the id of the row I just inserted",
      "port this PostgreSQL schema and these queries to Azure SQL Database",
      "why does LIMIT 10 not work"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "translate this query for our SQL database",
      "review this SQL before it goes in",
      "should I use ISNULL or COALESCE here"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "write an upsert that inserts or updates a row",
      "parse this JSON column with OPENJSON",
      "pick the column types and collation for this new table",
      "why is this query slow",
      "write a PostgreSQL migration for my Postgres database"
    ]
  }
}
